<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Esslingen am Neckar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Esslingen am <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a> is a city near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a> that grew around a shrine of Saint Vitalis and a bridge over the Neckar. A free imperial city from the 13th century until 1802, it was a leading member of the Swabian city leagues, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-league">Swabian League</a> was founded there in 1488. It passed to Württemberg in 1802 and became an early centre of industry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/esslingen-am-neckar.jpg" alt="Esslingen am Neckar" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Qwerty123uiop · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Esslingen_a.N._Fall_2023.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Esslingen am <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a> lies on the Neckar, about 14 kilometres south-east of the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, and is the seat of the District of Esslingen. Archaeological evidence shows settlement since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> period. In the 1st century CE the region became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. The place-name ending -ingen points to a settlement of a kinship group led by a man called Azzilo, Hezzilo or Hetsilo, and the name first appears in 856 as Ezlinga.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Esslingen is first mentioned in 777, in the will of Abbot Fulrad of Saint-Denis, chaplain of Pippin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>. He bequeathed to his monastery the sixth cell on the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a>, and he also brought the bones of Saint Vitalis to Esslingen, which made it a destination for pilgrims and led to its growth. Around 800 it became a market town, and it received city rights in 1229 under Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a> bridge, which still stands, made Esslingen a major centre of trade on the route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Taxes from the bridge and market, and exports of the region's wines, led to further growth. Between the 13th and 16th centuries the Free Imperial City was often in conflict with the Counts of Württemberg. About half the population died of famine or epidemics in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> between 1618 and 1648. In the Napoleonic era it lost its independence and became part of the Duchy of Württemberg.</p><p>Industrialisation in the 19th century, with glove manufacturing, textiles and metalworking, shaped the city's growth, and the first train ran from Cannstatt to Esslingen on 20 November 1845. US troops occupied the city in April 1945, and it suffered very little damage in the war, so the medieval character of its centre is largely preserved.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The old town keeps part of its defensive wall, including the Dicke Turm, or fat tower, above the vineyards, a market square ringed by half-timbered buildings, and the Altes Rathaus, the old city hall. The city lies on the German Timber-Frame Road and is the seat of the Esslingen University of Applied Sciences.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Esslingen am Neckar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Esslingen</b> grew around a shrine of Saint Vitalis and a bridge over the Neckar, gaining market rights in the Carolingian period.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-esslingen">Free Imperial City of Esslingen</a></li></ul></section>
